add(1,'exact spectrum support','Finite spectral rigidity needs annihilation and support witnesses','Exact linear algebra',
 'Which exact polynomial and boundary witnesses suffice to certify the seven-point spectrum?',
 'The July1 source reports a rational spectral-rigidity theorem with support0,1,2,4,5,6,8. Its original matrix and boundary seeds are not fully retrieved. A complete general certificate states exactly what those missing data would need to establish, and gives an explicit rational model rather than adopting an unseen computation.',
 ['Let S={0,1,2,4,5,6,8} and p(t)=∏_{λ∈S}(t−λ). Let T be an endomorphism of a finite-dimensional rational vector space. Define e_λ(t)=∏_{ν∈S,ν≠λ}(t−ν)/(λ−ν).'],
 'Lagrange spectral certificate',
 'If p(T)=0, then E_λ=e_λ(T) are pairwise disjoint idempotents, sum to I and satisfy T=ΣλE_λ. The spectrum equals S exactly when every E_λ is nonzero; one vector with E_λv≠0 certifies each nonzero projector.',
 'On the seven roots, e_λ(ν)=δ_λν. Thus e_λ²−e_λ, e_λe_ν for λ≠ν, Σe_λ−1 and t−Σλe_λ all vanish on S and are divisible by the square-free polynomial p. Evaluating at T proves the identities. The image of E_λ consists of λ-eigenvectors because(t−λ)e_λ is divisible by p. Their direct sum is the entire space, so p(T)=0 also proves semisimplicity rather than merely listing possible eigenvalues. Conversely a nonzero E_λ supplies an eigenvector, making the support criterion exact. A spectral list alone would not exclude a Jordan block, for which p(T) could fail.',
 'Take T=diag(0,1,2,4,5,6,8) and v=(1,1,1,1,1,1,1).',
 'Each E_λv is the corresponding standard basis vector. All seven eigenvalues therefore occur. The minimal polynomial is p, and seven independent boundary projections certify the full support without numerical diagonalization.',
 'An exact rational certificate is a polynomial identity plus explicit support witnesses, with no floating eigenvalue tolerance.',
 'This does not reproduce the reported Theorem C until its actual T and seed vectors are supplied. An arbitrary matrix sharing the spectrum need not share other structure.',
 'Publish T and sparse witness vectors, verify all polynomial identities over Q, and identify which hypotheses make this finite rigidity stable under a specified deformation.')
add(2,'finite-field quadratic jet target','Quadratic jets need their cubic closure to become finite','Commutative algebra',
 'What quotient is defined by the two quadrics, and what changes when all cubic monomials are added?',
 'The July3 target specifies two quadrics and a Hilbert function(1,2,1,0). The quadrics alone do not have that finite Hilbert function. An invertible coordinate change makes both the obstruction and the intended cubic closure completely explicit over every field.',
 ['Let k be any field and λ∈k. In k[a,b] set Q=(a²−λ²b²,ab−λb²). Let m=(a,b),u=a−λb and v=b. The coordinate change is invertible with a=u+λv.'],
 'Exact jet normal form',
 'Q=(u²,uv). Its quotient has Hilbert function1,2,1,1,1,… and is not Artinian. Adding m³ gives I=(u²,uv,v³), with basis1,u,v,v² and Hilbert function(1,2,1,0,…).',
 'The second quadric becomes uv. The first becomes u²+2λuv, so subtracting2λ times the second gives u²; this works even in characteristic two. Conversely these formulas recover the original generators. Every quotient monomial is then a power of v or the single monomial u; powers v^d survive in every degree. Adding all cubics kills v³, while u³,u²v,uv² were already killed. The four remaining monomials are independent because the ideal is monomial and none is divisible by u²,uv or v³. Thus the desired finite jet depends essentially on the cubic-closure hypothesis, not on a two-prime experimental agreement.',
 'Use λ=2 over Q or over F₇.',
 'With u=a−2b,v=b, the original quadrics are u²+4uv and uv. Both fields give the same four standard monomials after cubic closure. Without closure, v⁴ survives, contradicting the claimed terminal zero Hilbert coefficient.',
 'The coordinate calculation gives a characteristic-independent constructive model and states precisely which quotient is being studied.',
 'No larger W₁ containment or stage-N computation is inferred from this local normal form. Its relation to those packets needs an actual map and ideal certificate.',
 'Construct the map from the full target ideal to this jet model and return exact generator-reduction identities proving the intended cubic closure.')
add(3,'Hilbert–Burch resolution','The Hilbert–Burch quotient has type two despite its symmetric Hilbert function','Free resolutions',
 'What exact resolution and socle distinguish this quotient from a complete intersection?',
 'The July3 source correctly isolates I=(x²,xy,y³), Betti numbers(1,3,2) and socle dimension two. These can be proved directly, exposing why a short symmetric Hilbert function does not imply Gorenstein duality or a Pfaffian structure.',
 ['Let R=k[x,y],A=R/(x²,xy,y³). Its socle is{x∈A:(x,y)x=0}. Use φ=[[y,0],[−x,y²],[0,−x]], whose columns are syzygies among the ordered generators x²,xy,y³.'],
 'Exact minimal graded resolution',
 'There is a minimal resolution0→R(−3)⊕R(−4)→R(−2)²⊕R(−3)→R→A→0. A has basis1,x,y,y² and socle span{x,y²}, so its type is two.',
 'The two column relations are yx²−x(xy)=0 and y²(xy)−x(y³)=0. To prove they generate every syzygy, let ax²+bxy+cy³=0. Setting x=0 implies x divides c; write c=xc₁. Divide by x to get ax+by+c₁y³=0. Setting y=0 implies y divides a; write a=ya₁. Then b=−xa₁−y²c₁. This is a₁ times the first column plus−c₁ times the second. Injectivity of φ follows from ya₁=0 and−xc₂=0 in the domain R. All entries lie in the homogeneous maximal ideal, proving minimality. Multiplication by x,y on the four standard monomials gives the stated socle.',
 'Compare with B=R/(x²,y²).',
 'Both A and B have Hilbert function(1,2,1). But B has socle span{xy} and total Betti numbers(1,2,1), whereas A has a degree-one socle class x and type two. The Hilbert numerator of A has a cancellation at degree three that does not cancel a minimal free module.',
 'Resolution and socle data distinguish algebras that their length and Hilbert function cannot.',
 'This elementary proof concerns the declared ideal, not every reported finite-field target. Gorenstein and Pfaffian classifications need the appropriate codimension and type assumptions.',
 'Compute the multiplication pairing into the socle and identify the precise duality obstruction before proposing a higher-codimension structural theorem.')
add(4,'Pfaffian/Buchsbaum','Tensoring by a dual-number factor gives Betti numbers(1,4,5,2)','Homological algebra',
 'What happens to the exact resolution when an independent square-zero variable is adjoined?',
 'The source says the Pfaffian elevation was falsified and a tensor resolution substituted, but its last coefficients are truncated. A fully specified tensor extension can be computed exactly. It provides a constructive model without inventing the missing historical tail.',
 ['Let S=k[x,y,z] and B=S/(x²,xy,y³,z²)=A⊗_k k[z]/(z²), with A from the preceding ideal. Tensor the explicit two-step resolution of A with0→k[z](−2)→k[z]→k[z]/z²→0.'],
 'Exact tensor resolution and non-Gorenstein certificate',
 'B has total Betti numbers(1,4,5,2), length eight and Hilbert function(1,3,3,1). Its socle is span{xz,y²z}, of dimension two, so this codimension-three algebra is not Gorenstein.',
 'The two resolutions are complexes of free modules over disjoint polynomial variables. Over a field their tensor total complex resolves the tensor quotient; exactness follows degreewise from the exact tensor functor or the elementary Künneth theorem. All matrix entries remain in(x,y,z), so the total resolution is minimal. Its total ranks are the coefficient convolution(1+3t+2t²)(1+t)=1+4t+5t²+2t³. The eight basis monomials are the four A monomials multiplied by1 or z. A socle element must be killed by z and hence have a z factor; its A coefficient must lie in socle(A), giving exactly the two stated classes. The symmetric Hilbert series(1+t)³ therefore again fails to imply type one.',
 'Compare B with k[x,y,z]/(x²,y²,z²).',
 'The complete intersection has the same Hilbert function and length but Betti numbers(1,3,3,1) and one-dimensional socle xyz. The distinction is exact, not a finite-precision discrepancy.',
 'A tensor construction gives a rigorous replacement for an unsupported Gorenstein elevation.',
 'The displayed tensor extension is authored here; the original truncated packet is not silently identified with it. A codimension-three Gorenstein Pfaffian theorem cannot be invoked for this type-two quotient.',
 'Recover the actual third-variable relations, compare their minimal resolution with this model, and prove any flat-family claim over the relevant parameter ring.')
add(5,'two-prime return','Two-prime agreement is not a rational membership proof','Exact certification',
 'Which finite-field conclusions lift to Q, and what certificate proves an exponent or ideal containment?',
 'The July3 ladder reports N=7,N=9 and a pending N=11 exponent17 at two primes, while explicitly recording that Singular was not run in one response. Exact lifting logic distinguishes useful modular evidence from a rational theorem and gives a concrete certificate format.',
 ['Let p,q be distinct primes and M an integer matrix. A polynomial membership witness for f∈(g₁,…,g_s) over Q is an exact identity Df=Σh_ig_i with D a nonzero integer and h_i integral polynomials. Minimal nilpotence exponent also requires a valid nonmembership witness for the preceding power.'],
 'One-sided rank lifting and a two-prime counterexample',
 'rank_Fp(M modp)≤rank_QM. A nonzero modular minor proves the corresponding rational minor is nonzero. Vanishing modulo p and q alone does not prove rational vanishing.',
 'A modular nonzero determinant is the reduction of the same integer determinant, so that integer is nonzero; this proves the rank inequality. For the converse failure take the1×1 matrix[pq]. It is zero over both finite fields and nonzero over Q. Polynomial membership has an additional denominator issue: I=(px) in Z[x] becomes(x) over Q but becomes the zero ideal modulo p. Thus a failed modular membership test may also be a bad-prime phenomenon unless a monic Gröbner basis or a flat integral model controls specialization. An exact identity Df=Σh_ig_i avoids ambiguity and lifts directly after dividing by D. To claim the smallest exponent e, also prove f^(e−1) has nonzero normal form under a certified rational Gröbner basis.',
 'Use p=32003,q=40009.',
 'The integer1280408027=pq vanishes at both primes but is not zero. Conversely the monomial quotient(x²,xy,y³) has a free integral standard-monomial basis, so its Hilbert function is genuinely identical after every prime reduction.',
 'Modular computations can guide discovery while the final certificate identifies exactly what is proved over Q.',
 'The reported stage exponents and full containment remain historical computational claims until their actual generators and certificates are recovered.',
 'Publish exact polynomial identities or rational Gröbner certificates, with prime exclusions and coefficient bounds if CRT reconstruction is used.')
add(6,'GF(2) centralizers','Pauli centralizers are exact symplectic kernels','Finite quantum algebra',
 'Which binary matrix computes the Pauli symmetries of a declared Hamiltonian?',
 'The July4 sector calculations have a precise finite-field core. Once the actual nonzero Pauli support is specified, its centralizer follows from one symplectic nullspace. This constructive theorem states what the reported chemical, spin and lattice dimensions would need to certify without adopting unseen matrices.',
 ['Encode an n-qubit Pauli up to phase by v=(x,z)∈F₂^(2n). Define ω(v,w)=x·z′+z·x′. Let H=Σ_{s∈S}h_sP(s), with distinct support labels and all h_s≠0.'],
 'Centralizer formula',
 'P(v) commutes with H exactly when ω(v,s)=0 for every s∈S. Thus C(H)=span(S)^⊥ has dimension2n−rank_F2(S).',
 'The single-qubit relation XZ=−ZX gives P(v)P(s)=(−1)^ω(v,s)P(s)P(v). If all pairings vanish, each term commutes. Conversely a nonzero commutator is a sum of nonzero multiples of Pauli matrices labeled v+s for the anticommuting support labels. Those labels are distinct, so linear independence of the Pauli basis prevents cancellation. Therefore all pairings must vanish. The symplectic form is nondegenerate, so the annihilator dimension is the ambient dimension minus the support span dimension. This proof also explains why one must first combine duplicate Pauli terms and remove coefficients that cancel to zero.',
 'Take n=2,H=Z₁+Z₂.',
 'The support equations force x₁=x₂=0 and leave z₁,z₂ free. The Pauli centralizer has dimension two and consists of I,Z₁,Z₂,Z₁Z₂ up to phase. Replacing H by X₁+Z₁+X₂+Z₂ makes the four binary support directions span everything, so the Pauli centralizer is zero-dimensional.',
 'Exact GF₂ linear algebra yields the symmetry space; interpreting it as a commuting sector algebra additionally requires an isotropic choice.',
 'A Pauli centralizer is not the entire real or complex operator commutant. The source’s particular Hamiltonian support tables were not recovered, so their dimension reports remain qualified.',
 'Publish each support matrix and row-reduction certificate, then select and certify a maximal isotropic commuting subgroup with the intended phase conventions.')
add(7,'sector–abelian quotient','Sector transition counts use the symplectic-kernel quotient','Representation sectors',
 'Which quotient counts distinct character shifts, and when does the source’s dimension formula apply?',
 'The source reports2^a sectors from a quotient involving G and Q. Quotient cardinality is elementary, but identifying it with character transitions requires the correct kernel. The exact transition theorem both proves the constructive part and identifies an assumption that cannot be omitted.',
 ['Let Q be an r-dimensional isotropic binary Pauli subspace with independent commuting Hermitian generators. Its joint eigenspaces are H_χ,χ∈F₂^r. For v define δ_v(q)=ω(v,q). Let G be any binary subspace of possible Pauli interventions.'],
 'Character transport and orbit cardinality',
 'P(v)H_χ=H_(χ+δ_v). The number of reachable character shifts is2^rankδ=2^[dimG−dim(G∩Q^⊥)]. Replacing Q^⊥ by Q requires the extra identity G∩Q^⊥=G∩Q.',
 'If ψ has eigenvalue(−1)^χ(q) under P(q), commuting P(q) past P(v) multiplies it by(−1)^ω(q,v), giving the shifted character. P(v) is invertible, so this is an equality of eigenspaces. The shift map G→Q* is linear with kernel G∩Q^⊥; rank-nullity proves its image cardinality. Independently, every joint character sector has dimension2^(n−r): its projector is the product of r factors(I+(−1)^χ_jP(q_j))/2, and all nonidentity Pauli terms have trace zero. A formula using dimG−dim(G∩Q) counts a different vector-space quotient unless the kernels agree.',
 'Use Q=span{Z₁} and G=span{Z₁,Z₂}.',
 'Every element of G commutes with Q, so all character shifts are zero and only one sector is reachable. Yet dimG−dim(G∩Q)=1 would predict two if misinterpreted. For the full Pauli space and a maximal Lagrangian Q the required kernel identity does hold.',
 'The genuine theorem is a symplectic transition statement, with sector counts tied to explicit hypotheses.',
 'The historical quotient may have a different intended meaning; its exact definition was not recovered. No reported sector dimension is silently rejected or accepted without that identification.',
 'Recover the actual G,Q pair, compute both intersections and demonstrate whether the desired quotient is the character-shift quotient.')
add(8,'sector–Schmidt ranks','Sector Schmidt ranks add only across orthogonal blocks','Tensor linear algebra',
 'Which block structure justifies a sector rank sum3+2=5, and what happens to entropy?',
 'The July4 source reports a sector–Schmidt split3+2. The exact algebra is a block singular-value decomposition when the sectors are orthogonal on both tensor factors. Orthogonality on just one side does not suffice to add ranks.',
 ['Represent a bipartite pure state by its coefficient matrix C. Assume orthogonal decompositions on both sides put C into block diagonal form diag(C₁,…,C_s). Normalize||C||_F²=1 and set p_j=||C_j||_F².'],
 'Block Schmidt decomposition',
 'rankC=ΣrankC_j. Its reduced-state spectrum is the union of the spectra C_jC_j*, and its entropy is H(p)+Σp_jS(C_jC_j*/p_j), omitting zero blocks.',
 'Block multiplication gives CC*=diag(C₁C₁*,…,C_sC_s*), so eigenvalues and ranks are unions. Write each nonzero block spectrum as p_jλ_{jk} withΣ_kλ_{jk}=1. Then−Σ_jk p_jλ_jk log(p_jλ_jk)=−Σ_jp_jlogp_j+Σ_jp_j[−Σ_kλ_jklogλ_jk], proving the entropy identity. If only one-side sectors are orthogonal, C may instead be a vertical stack with overlapping row spaces; its rank can be smaller than the sum. Thus a global charge label alone must be translated into the relevant local block structure before the formula is used.',
 'Take C=I₅/√5 split into a3×3 and2×2 block.',
 'The Schmidt rank is five. Sector probabilities are3/5 and2/5 and the entropy is H(3/5,2/5)+(3/5)log3+(2/5)log2=log5. In contrast stacking two identical one-row matrices gives rank one, despite a sum of individual ranks two.',
 'The rank sum and entropy decomposition now have a complete hypothesis and proof.',
 'The actual H_spin coefficient blocks were not retrieved. This is the exact theorem their reported decomposition would need, not a reproduction of the original state.',
 'Return orthogonal local sector projectors and coefficient blocks, then certify their singular ranks and entropy bounds with exact or interval data.')
add(9,'cubic field discriminant','The discriminant−23 cubic and the plastic unit are exactly related','Algebraic number theory',
 'Which field, unit and Galois group follow from t³−t²+1?',
 'The July5 knot-arithmetic discussion begins with a fully defined cubic. Its discriminant, signature and relation to the plastic number can be proved exactly. These facts do not themselves identify a hyperbolic structure, regulator normalization or Mahler-measure formula.',
 ['Let f(t)=t³−t²+1 and let t₀ be its real root. Let ρ>1 solveρ³−ρ−1=0. The polynomial discriminant of t³+at²+bt+c is a²b²−4b³−4a³c−27c²+18abc.'],
 'Exact field and unit data',
 'f is irreducible,Disc(f)=−23, and Q(t₀)=Q(ρ) with t₀=−1/ρ. The field has one real and one complex-conjugate pair of embeddings;ρ is a norm-one algebraic unit. The splitting-field Galois group is S₃.',
 'The only possible rational roots are±1, and neither vanishes. Substituting a=−1,b=0,c=1 gives4−27=−23. Its square-free discriminant implies the index of Z[t₀] in the full integer ring is one, since an index square must divide23. Negative discriminant gives signature(1,1). Substituting t=−1/ρ turns f(t) into(ρ³−ρ−1)/ρ³, proving the field relation. The monic polynomial forρ has constant−1, so its norm is one and it is a unit. An irreducible cubic has transitive subgroup A₃ or S₃; its nonsquare discriminant excludes A₃. Modulo two f is irreducible, while modulo five it factors(t−2)(t²+t+2) with irreducible quadratic, independently exhibiting3-cycle and transposition types.',
 'Compare the real root and unit numerically only after the exact relation.',
 'ρ≈1.324718 and t₀≈−0.754878. Their product is−1. The complex roots of f have modulus√ρ because their product isρ. Calling logρ the full regulator additionally requires provingρ is a fundamental unit; unit status alone is insufficient.',
 'The exact cubic arithmetic is separated from every further knot or period identification.',
 'No atlas of28 volume identities or p-adic regulator is reproduced. Those require the actual shapes, branches and arithmetic normalization.',
 'Certify a fundamental unit and an integral basis, then derive any proposed regulator or knot trace-field map with explicit comparison data.',[ANT,('LMFDB number field3.1.23.1','https://www.lmfdb.org/NumberField/3.1.23.1')])
add(10,'eta(\\tau)\\eta(23','The eta product has exact coefficients and a Fricke law','Modular analysis',
 'Which transformation identities can be proved for η(τ)η(23τ) before invoking a knot correspondence?',
 'The July5 source couples an eta product to cubic and knot data. Its q-expansion and two functional equations are independently meaningful classical mathematics. They provide genuine consistency tests while leaving a full geometric correspondence unproved.',
 ['For Imτ>0 set q=e^(2πiτ) and η(τ)=q^(1/24)∏_{n≥1}(1−q^n). Use η(τ+1)=e^(πi/12)η(τ) and η(−1/τ)=√(−iτ)η(τ), with the standard holomorphic square-root branch. Put F=η(τ)η(23τ).'],
 'Product and Fricke identities',
 'F=q∏(1−q^n)(1−q^(23n)),F(τ+1)=F(τ), and F(−1/(23τ))=−i√23 τF(τ). Its first coefficients are q−q²−q³+q⁶+q⁸+….',
 'The q-prefactors multiply to q^[(1+23)/24]=q. Translation multiplies the first eta by e^(πi/12) and the second by e^(23πi/12), whose product is one. For the Fricke substitution apply the inversion formula to each factor, obtaining√(−i23τ)√(−iτ)=−i√23τ. The branch equality holds on the positive imaginary axis and hence throughout the connected upper half-plane by analytic continuation. Below degree24 the23n product contributes only its constant term, so finite expansion of∏(1−q^n), or Euler’s pentagonal identity, gives the displayed coefficients. These calculations are exact, not recognized from a numerical plot.',
 'Evaluate the Fricke multiplier at its fixed pointτ=i/√23.',
 'The multiplier−i√23τ is one, consistent with a nonzero product there. The coefficient at q² is−1 and at q⁵ is zero, matching the small-prime S₃ trace pattern only after a separate modular/Artin theorem is specified.',
 'The analytic object has a concrete domain, branch convention and verified transformations.',
 'Two generator-type identities do not by themselves prove the entire Γ₀(23) multiplier law, an Artin correspondence or a knot-volume identity. Those need their own theorem and normalization.',
 'Prove the complete eta-quotient modularity and character, then identify the associated representation using exact Hecke data and a suitable uniqueness theorem.',[('NIST DLMF eta transformations','https://dlmf.nist.gov/23.18')])
add(11,'graph spectrum','The five-vertex spectrum reconstructs a graph exactly','Spectral graph theory',
 'Which graph invariants and uniqueness statement follow from the reported spectrum?',
 'The July5 spectrum has a complete finite interpretation. Under the simple-graph hypothesis it identifies a five-vertex graph uniquely up to isomorphism. A separate graph-to-knot assignment is still required before this becomes a knot invariant.',
 ['Let A be the adjacency matrix of a simple undirected graph on five vertices with spectrum{1+√5,1−√5,−2,0,0}. For such graphs trA²=2|E| and trA³=6 times the triangle count.'],
 'A small spectral-rigidity result',
 'The graph is K₅ with two disjoint edges removed. Its characteristic polynomial is t²(t+2)(t²−2t−4)=t⁵−8t³−8t².',
 'The squared eigenvalues sum to16, so there are eight edges, exactly two fewer than K₅. Their cubes sum to24, so there are four triangles. Deleting two adjacent edges from K₅ destroys five triangles, leaving five, because the deleted edges share one triangle. Deleting disjoint edges destroys six, leaving four. Thus the two missing edges must be disjoint, proving uniqueness. Conversely pair the missing-edge endpoints into two groups of size two and leave the fifth vertex alone. Differences within either pair give two zero eigenvectors. The difference between the pair-constant vectors gives eigenvalue−2. On the remaining pair-symmetric/central subspace the quotient matrix[[2,1],[4,0]] has eigenvalues1±√5. This constructs the spectrum rather than only checking moments.',
 'Take the missing edges12 and34.',
 'The graph has eight edges and four triangles. Multiplying the displayed factors gives t⁵−8t³−8t², independently consistent with the edge and triangle traces. Other graphs can be cospectral in larger orders; the uniqueness here uses the complete five-vertex classification after fixing the edge deficit.',
 'The spectrum supports an exact graph theorem with a small transparent proof.',
 'The source did not recover the map assigning this graph to5₂ or prove invariance under diagram moves. Spectral uniqueness of a graph is not uniqueness of a knot.',
 'Define the graph assignment, check its behavior under the intended diagram moves and compare which graph invariants actually descend to knot invariants.')
add(12,'Borel–Zagier','Numerical volume closure needs a geometric certificate','Hyperbolic geometry',
 'What turns algebraic shape parameters and a small numerical residual into a volume identity?',
 'The July5 atlas reports extremely small Mahler-volume discrepancies and rational-looking normalized values. Those remain historical computational reports without their shape and integration certificates. A constructive local-to-global criterion explains the missing mathematical work.',
 ['An ideal hyperbolic tetrahedron is determined by a shape z in the upper half-plane. Its volume is the Bloch–Wigner function D(z)=ImLi₂(z)+arg(1−z)log|z|. A triangulated complete manifold requires edge gluing and cusp-completeness equations, not just a root of one polynomial.'],
 'Finite-cover volume ratios and certified-shape scope',
 'If two finite-volume hyperbolic manifolds have a common finite cover with degrees m,n, then Vol(M)/Vol(N)=n/m. A collection of certified positively oriented complete shapes permits summing their tetrahedral volumes; an approximate shape list alone does not prove the topology or completeness.',
 'A covering is a local isometry, so the volume form pulls back and integration over the cover multiplies volume by its degree. Thus mVol(M)=nVol(N), proving the rational ratio. Restricting the ratio further to powers of two and three would require corresponding index or arithmetic information. For shapes, gluing equations identify the tetrahedral metrics along faces and ensure the angle/product condition around edges; cusp completeness is the additional condition preventing incomplete peripheral geometry. These hypotheses, plus a validated solution and the correct triangulated manifold, are what permit the volume sum. Numerical agreement at38 digits does not establish them or an equality involving a Mahler-measure integral.',
 'Consider a common cover of degrees4 over M and9 over N.',
 'The ratio is9/4 by integration, with no numerical recognition. This is only an illustrative index calculation; it does not assert that5₂ has such a cover or is arithmetic. Complex conjugating every shape reverses D’s sign, demonstrating the importance of orientation and branches.',
 'Exact geometric and analytic premises are needed before the atlas’s normalization can become a theorem.',
 'The original28-row data, A-polynomial, quadrature enclosure and shape certificates were not retrieved. No equalityπm(A)=Vol or specific normalized Θ value is adopted as proved here.',
 'Recover the exact triangulation and polynomials, verify complete shapes with intervals, and enclose the independent Mahler integral before comparing both sides.',[('SnapPy verified computations','https://snappy.computop.org/verify.html')])
add(13,'Hadamard parametrices','A parametrix isolates a singularity and a smooth remainder','Differential equations',
 'What is a concrete singular model, and how does it differ from a complete inverse or a propagation theorem?',
 'The July6 discussion emphasized singularities, Green functions and parametrix thinking. A one-dimensional model proves the central idea exactly. A wave example then distinguishes stationary elliptic singularities from hyperbolic propagation without importing an unspecified microlocal framework.',
 ['Let L=−d²/dx²+1 on R and G(x)=e^(−|x|)/2. Choose smooth compactly supported χ equal to one near zero. Distributional derivatives include the jump in G′ at zero.'],
 'A local inverse with a smooth error',
 'LG=δ₀ and L(χG)=δ₀+r, where r=−χ″G−2χ′G′ is smooth and supported away from zero. Thus convolution by χG is a local parametrix with smoothing remainder.',
 'For x≠0, G″=G. Its first derivative jumps from1/2 to−1/2, so distributionally G″=G−δ₀. This proves the fundamental-solution identity. The product rule gives the cutoff formula; since derivatives of χ vanish near zero, the potentially nonsmooth factors are only used where G is smooth, making r smooth. Convolution with the smooth compactly supported remainder smooths compactly supported distributions. This is the exact decomposition into a universal local singularity and a regular error. By contrast the1+1-dimensional wave equation transports initial singularities: with initial displacement f and zero velocity, u(t,x)=[f(x−t)+f(x+t)]/2. A step in f produces characteristic fronts x=±t rather than a singularity fixed at x=0.',
 'Use f=1_(x≥0) for the wave comparison.',
 'The solution has jumps along two lightlike lines, while G is continuous with a derivative jump only at the source point. The different differential operators dictate the propagation law.',
 'A precise parametrix is an operator identity with a controlled remainder, not merely a metaphor for approximation.',
 'This elementary example does not construct a curved-space Hadamard parametrix, a wavefront-set calculus or an elliptic-cohomology invariant.',
 'For a specified variable-coefficient operator, construct the local singular kernel, prove remainder regularity and derive the correct propagation relation.')
add(14,'half-spin representation dimension','The120+128 decomposition is a dimension and weight calculation','Lie theory',
 'Why do so₁₆ and a half-spin module contribute248 dimensions, and what additional structure makes the sum into e₈?',
 'The July6 source highlights the exact vector-space decomposition e₈≅so₁₆⊕S⁺. Its dimensions and root weights can be computed concretely. A direct sum of vector spaces is not automatically a Lie algebra; the mixed and spin-spin brackets remain essential.',
 ['For so₂n the dimension is n(2n−1). Its complex spin module can be modeled onΛ* C^n, split into even and odd exterior degree. For n=8 each half-spin space has dimension2⁷.'],
 'Exact dimension and E₈ root accounting',
 'dim so₁₆=120 and dimS⁺=128, totaling248. The112 D₈ roots±e_i±e_j plus128 even-parity half-integral weights give the240 roots of E₈; adding rank eight gives its Lie-algebra dimension.',
 'Skew16×16 matrices have16·15/2=120 independent entries. The exterior algebra on eight generators has dimension2⁸; toggling one generator pairs even and odd subsets, so each parity half has128. D₈ roots choose two of eight positions and four signs, giving4C(8,2)=112. The half-spin weights are(±1/2,…,±1/2) with even minus parity, again128. Every displayed root has squared length two, and the count is112+128=240. Constructing e₈ additionally requires the so₁₆ action onS⁺ and a compatible equivariant bracket[S⁺,S⁺]→so₁₆ satisfying Jacobi; dimension arithmetic alone does not supply those maps.',
 'Compare the half-spin count with the full spin representation.',
 'The full spin space has256 dimensions, so120+256 would give376, not248. Choosing one chirality is substantive. The number128 is therefore a representation dimension under stated algebraic hypotheses, not evidence for a physical hidden dimension.',
 'The count explains the precise decomposition while preserving the distinction between module data and a Lie bracket.',
 'No anomaly cancellation, tmf orientation or physical field content follows from the count. Such interpretations need independent structures and theorems.',
 'Write the equivariant spin-spin bracket explicitly and verify Jacobi on generators before using the decomposition in a geometric or physical model.',[('Etingof, Lie groups and Lie algebras','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-745-lie-groups-and-lie-algebras-i-fall-2020/')])
add(15,'identity energy','A derivative residual can be an identity rather than a prediction','Model identifiability',
 'What is tested when the residual subtracts quantities already tied by the assumed differential law?',
 'The July6 mass-ratio campaign reported very high identity-energy R² values. Those reports require the unrecovered dataset for empirical assessment. A direct differentiation theorem separates a valid invariant residual from a circular fit or an independent predictive test.',
 ['Let y_i,x_a be differentiable functions ofℓ, with fixed coefficients q_i,κ_i. Define z_i=d(y_i−q_ix_{a_i})/dℓ. Assume the model y_i′=κ_iγ_m+ε_i. A change of fixed units adds constants to the logarithmic variables.'],
 'Exact residual decomposition and unit invariance',
 'z_i=κ_iγ_m−q_ix′_{a_i}+ε_i. Fixed additive unit constants cancel from derivatives. If q_i varies, an extra−q_i′x_{a_i} term is mandatory.',
 'Differentiate the definition using the product rule, then substitute the model equation. This proves the decomposition without regression. Replacing a logarithm y_i by y_i+c_i and x_a by x_a+d_a leaves derivatives unchanged, proving the unit statement. If a comparison target is built from the same assumed derivative identity, its small residual tests internal algebra or numerical consistency, not independent explanatory power. A held-out target can be meaningful only if its data, coefficients and feature choices were fixed independently of the test. Very high R² alone does not reveal which of these situations occurred.',
 'Take x(ℓ)=ℓ,y(ℓ)=3ℓ+7,q=2,κ=3,γ_m=1.',
 'Both sides give z=1 exactly. Adding100 to y or subtracting4 from x changes nothing. If q(ℓ)=2+ℓ, however,z=d[3ℓ+7−(2+ℓ)ℓ]/dℓ=1−2ℓ; omitting q′ would incorrectly give1−ℓ.',
 'The invariant derivative is a genuine mathematical fact, while its empirical interpretation needs a separate test design.',
 'The source’s grid, target labels and reported prediction errors are not reproduced or endorsed. No particle-mass or running-coupling law is inferred.',
 'Freeze coefficients and data provenance, distinguish identity checks from independent predictions, and derive a stated uncertainty model for every differentiation step.')
add(16,'Phase II.8 built','Finite positive atoms produce a Herglotz function, but differences need a new proof','Complex analysis',
 'Which positivity statement follows from positive Clark-type masses, and which residual positivity does not?',
 'The July Clark campaign reports a finite symmetric atomic measure and a residual positivity atlas. The atomic transform has a complete elementary proof. It must be separated from a difference of transforms and from the unbounded-height theorem required by the broader RH programme.',
 ['Let λ_k>0 be distinct and μ₀,μ_k>0. For Imz>0 define M(z)=−μ₀/z+Σ_{k=1}^N2μ_kz/(λ_k²−z²). A Herglotz function is analytic in the upper half-plane with positive imaginary part.'],
 'Finite atomic positivity and real interlacing',
 'M is Herglotz. Its real poles are0,±λ_k, with negative residues of magnitudesμ₀,μ_k. Between successive poles M is strictly increasing and has exactly one zero.',
 'Rewrite each symmetric summand asμ_k/(λ_k−z)+μ_k/(−λ_k−z). Every atom μ/(t−z) has imaginary partμ Imz/|t−z|²>0. Their finite sum proves analyticity and positivity. On the real line away from poles, M′(x)=Σ_t μ_t/(t−x)²>0. Immediately right of a pole the function tends−∞ and immediately left of the next it tends+∞, so monotonicity gives exactly one zero. But the class of Herglotz functions is not closed under subtraction: M₁=−1/z and M₂=−2/z are both Herglotz, while M₁−M₂=1/z has negative imaginary part. Therefore positivity of a reported residual requires its exact definition and a separate certificate.',
 'Take one symmetric pairλ=2 and all three masses one.',
 'M=−1/z+2z/(4−z²). At z=i its imaginary part is1+2/5=7/5. Its real zeros solve−4+3x²=0, giving±2/√3 between the three poles−2,0,2.',
 'Positive atomic weights yield a rigorous finite function theorem, not automatically every claimed remainder or global zeta statement.',
 'The source’s137-atom data and interval atlases are historical reports unless their certificates are supplied. A finite-height result does not control the T→∞ tail or prove RH.',
 'Recover the exact residual sign convention and decompose its representing measure, or verify a uniform analytic lower bound including every omitted tail term.',[('Baranov et al., Spectral synthesis in de Branges spaces','https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.6915')])
add(17,'key target','Monotone phase gives interlacing roots and positive local masses','Spectral analysis',
 'How does a strict phase derivative imply root counting, analytic continuation and residue positivity?',
 'The July13–14 interpretation correctly distinguishes B-zeros from direct zeta-zero tracking. A phase theorem makes that distinction exact. It also states the nonvanishing and derivative hypotheses needed before a numerical root family becomes a certified analytic curve.',
 ['Let E_α(x)=A_α(x)−iB_α(x)=r_α(x)e^(−iφ_α(x)), with r>0 and real-analytic data on a rectangle. Suppose∂_xφ≥c>0. Put m=−A/B wherever B≠0.'],
 'Level curves, interlacing and local atomic weight',
 'B-zeros occur atφ=kπ and A-zeros atφ=π/2+kπ, so they are simple and interlace. Each B-root continues analytically with λ′=−φ_α/φ_x. Its local Herglotz-normalized mass is A(λ)/B′(λ)=1/φ_x(λ)>0.',
 'The polar form gives A=r cosφ and B=r sinφ. Strict phase monotonicity makes each level have at most one root and gives the alternating order of the two level families. At a B-root, B′=rφ_x cosφ is nonzero, proving simplicity; the analytic implicit-function theorem then gives the parameter derivative. Dividing A by B′ cancels rcosφ and gives the mass. Near λ, m(z)=−[A(λ)/B′(λ)]/(z−λ)+an analytic term, matching a positive atomμ/(λ−z). None of this asserts global Herglotz positivity from local residues alone; an entire upper-half-plane condition is stronger.',
 'Useφ(x)=πx and inspect0<x<137.75.',
 'There are137 positive B-levels at1,…,137 and138 positive A-levels at1/2,3/2,…,137.5. The central B-level is zero. Every local mass is1/π. These exact counts show how the source’s137/138 pattern can arise from phase levels without identifying them with zeros of another function.',
 'Root counts and masses follow from a uniform phase theorem with explicit boundaries.',
 'The actual ξ logarithmic-derivative enclosure is not reconstructed here. Endpoints crossing a level change the count, and E=0 would invalidate the polar representation.',
 'Publish a nonvanishing enclosure and a positive derivative lower bound over the full rectangle, then continue every labeled level with validated endpoint control.')
add(18,'adaptive subdivision','A positivity atlas needs a covering bound, not a dense scatterplot','Validated analysis',
 'What local inequality certifies positivity on an entire parameter box, and how should anisotropic boxes be chosen?',
 'The July16–18 continuation used moving centers, Taylor models and adaptive subdivision. A finite-box theorem identifies the essential certificate. It explains both the legitimate advantage of anisotropy and why the reported center values or45-point checks alone cannot prove a continuum claim.',
 ['Let f be continuously differentiable on a box centered at c with radii r_i. Suppose an enclosure gives f(c)≥m and uniform coordinate derivative bounds|∂_if|≤L_i. All quantities are rigorous bounds, not point samples.'],
 'A box positivity certificate and an optimal first-order shape',
 'If m−ΣL_ir_i>0, then f>0 everywhere in the box. With positive L_i and a fixed budgetΣL_ir_i≤M, the unconstrained maximum-volume box has r_i=M/(dL_i).',
 'Integrate f along the coordinate-segment path from c to any x in the box. The absolute change is at mostΣL_i|x_i−c_i|≤ΣL_ir_i, proving the lower bound. For volume, set a_i=L_ir_i. AM–GM gives∏a_i≤(M/d)^d, with equality when every a_i=M/d. Thus the longest certified direction is the one with the smallest derivative bound. Higher Taylor models can replace the linear budget, but then their coefficient and remainder enclosures must remain uniform throughout each cell. A union of certified boxes proves the global statement only if it really covers the intended domain and every center’s certificate uses the correct parameter/base point.',
 'Use d=2,m=1,L₁=10,L₂=1 and budget M=1/2.',
 'The optimal radii are r₁=1/40,r₂=1/4. The lower bound is1/2. An isotropic radius constrained by the same budget is1/22, whose area is substantially smaller. The numerical gain follows from the derivative geometry, not an intrinsic superiority of a named jet architecture.',
 'The proof separates predictor accuracy, validated local remainder and global cover completeness.',
 'No original271-cell atlas, synchronized centers or derivative bounds were recovered as executable certificates. A historical PASS count is not substituted for them.',
 'Export each cell, exact center, lower margin and uniform derivative/Taylor bounds; independently verify both its local inequality and the union’s coverage.')
add(19,'B=\\alpha\\widehat B','The small-alpha frontier is a critical-point normal form','Analytic perturbation',
 'Why can B-roots approach critical points rather than zeros, and which renormalized mass has a finite limit?',
 'The July18 frontier discussion proposes B=αBhat and finiteαμ_k. A real-analytic normal form proves this behavior under precise nondegeneracy assumptions. It also shows that the limiting mass sign is a substantive condition, not automatic from formal scaling.',
 ['Let F be real on a real interval and holomorphic nearby. Set E_α(x)=F(x+iα)=A_α(x)−iB_α(x). Let x₀ satisfy F′(x₀)=0,F″(x₀)≠0 and F(x₀)≠0.'],
 'Critical-point continuation and renormalized mass',
 'For smallα>0 there is a B-root λ(α)=x₀+[F‴(x₀)/(6F″(x₀))]α²+O(α⁴). With μ=A(λ)/B′(λ), αμ→−F(x₀)/F″(x₀).',
 'Taylor expansion gives A=F−α²F″/2+O(α⁴) and B=−αF′+α³F‴/6+O(α⁵). Divide B byα and useβ=α². Its derivative in x at(0,x₀) is−F″≠0, so the analytic implicit-function theorem supplies a root analytic inβ. Comparing theβ coefficient gives the shift. Also B′(λ)=−αF″(x₀)+O(α³), while A(λ)=F(x₀)+O(α²), proving the mass limit. It is positive precisely when F and F″ have opposite signs at the critical point. Multiple critical points or F(x₀)=0 require a different scaling.',
 'Take F(x)=1−x².',
 'Then A=1−x²+α² and B=2αx. The root stays at zero,μ=(1+α²)/(2α), andαμ→1/2, exactly−F(0)/F″(0). The unscaled mass diverges although its renormalization is regular.',
 'A proved normal form explains the singular scale without converting a finite-height continuation into a global theorem.',
 'The example is newly chosen. Applying it to Ξ requires the stated analytic and nondegeneracy hypotheses at every relevant critical point; an unbounded-height uniform limit is not established.',
 'Certify the nondegenerate critical points and uniform Taylor remainders, then determine whether the renormalized family extends continuously or analytically to the full boundary.')
add(20,'padded degree-5','Moment inversion must preserve the root count and its conditioning','Polynomial reconstruction',
 'What information do the power sums determine, and why can padding create tiny complex roots?',
 'The July20 cluster reported three roots and a padded degree-five inversion with a tiny complex pair. Newton identities explain an exact failure mode. It is a reconstruction artifact unless the analytic root count and moment enclosures independently justify the larger degree.',
 ['For N roots r_j, power sums are s_k=Σr_j^k, including s₀=N. Newton identities recursively give ke_k=Σ_{i=1}^k(−1)^(i−1)e_{k−i}s_i,e₀=1. The monic polynomial is t^N−e₁t^(N−1)+⋯+(−1)^Ne_N.'],
 'Exact reconstruction and padding instability',
 'The first N positive power sums and the correct N determine the polynomial. Multiplying it by t^m leaves every positive power sum unchanged but adds m zero roots; ignoring s₀ therefore loses degree information.',
 'Newton’s recursion derives by comparing the logarithmic derivative of∏(1+r_jz) with its power-series expansion, or by multiplying the root equations and collecting symmetric terms. Appending zero roots changes no s_k for k≥1 but changes s₀, proving the ambiguity. Repeated padded zeros are ill-conditioned: t(t²−1)(t²+ε) differs from the padded polynomial t³(t²−1) by coefficients of orderε but has an extra pair±i√ε. A small coefficient or moment error can therefore produce a much larger imaginary displacement. A separate transport law multiplying a moment error by4^m amplifies its absolute enclosure by that factor unless correlations are explicitly retained.',
 'Use original roots−1,0,1.',
 'Their moments are s₀=3,s₁=0,s₂=2,s₃=0. Degree three yields t³−t. Degree five with the same positive moments introduces extra zeros. Atε=10⁻⁸ the perturbed padded factor gives imaginary roots±10⁻⁴i, despite coefficient changes of size10⁻⁸.',
 'A complex pair in a padded reconstruction is not evidence of off-axis zeros of the original analytic function.',
 'The source’s moment quadrature and zero counts were not recovered as validated intervals. No reported zeta ordinate is certified here.',
 'First certify the analytic number of roots by an argument-principle contour, then solve the correctly sized moment problem with interval-aware conditioning bounds.')
add(21,'Smith normal','Integral homology retains torsion that rational ranks erase','Homological algebra',
 'How do integer normal forms and the boundary–coboundary pairing turn a topological calculation into a checkable certificate?',
 'The recovered Morphonic discussion includes integral homology, Smith normal form and discrete Stokes. Their constructive content is an integer chain complex together with unimodular change-of-basis witnesses. Rational ranks recover Betti numbers, but cannot by themselves recover finite torsion.',
 ['A chain complex consists of free abelian groups C_k and integer matrices ∂_k satisfying ∂_k∂_{k+1}=0. Its homology is ker∂_k/im∂_{k+1}. A unimodular matrix has determinant±1 and an integer inverse. Cochains C^k=Hom(C_k,Z) have coboundary δφ=φ∘∂.'],
 'Integer cokernel and discrete Stokes certificates',
 'If PMQ=diag(d₁,…,d_r,0), with P,Q unimodular and d_i dividing d_{i+1}, then cokerM≅⊕Z/d_i⊕Z^{m−r}. For every cochainφ and chainc, ⟨δφ,c⟩=⟨φ,∂c⟩ exactly.',
 'Changing domain basis by Q and codomain basis by P preserves the image quotient. For a diagonal matrix the quotient separates coordinate by coordinate, proving the cokernel formula directly. The invariant factors arise from integer Euclidean row and column operations; floating elimination cannot replace their divisibility data. The pairing identity follows immediately from the definition of δ, so discrete Stokes is compatible with the actual integer boundary operator rather than a numerical approximation. For general homology, first compute an integer basis of ker∂_k, express im∂_{k+1} in that basis, and take its cokernel. Applying Smith form to an unrelated boundary matrix without this kernel step can give the wrong group.',
 'Take M=[[2,4],[6,8]], P=[[1,0],[3,−1]] and Q=[[1,−2],[0,1]]. Also consider the cellular complex of RP².',
 'Both P and Q are unimodular and PMQ=diag(2,4), hence cokerM≅Z/2⊕Z/4. For RP² the complex is Z --2→ Z --0→ Z, giving H₁=Z/2, H₂=0 and H₀=Z. Over Q its first homology vanishes: the torsion was erased by changing coefficients.',
 'The integer matrix products and divisibility conditions provide a compact independently replayable certificate.',
 'The source did not supply all original cellular boundary matrices. These examples establish the method, not a homology computation for an unidentified object.',
 'Return boundary matrices, integer kernel bases and unimodular normal-form witnesses for every proposed complex, then compare integral persistence with rational persistence.')
add(22,'sheaf','A rank-one cellular sheaf on a circle is controlled by holonomy','Cellular sheaves',
 'Which global sections and first cohomology survive a nontrivial rank-one transport around a cycle?',
 'The late-July source connects cellular sheaves, cohomology and compositional consistency. A circle with scalar transports gives a complete exact example in which a topological cycle and a nonzero cohomology class are distinct questions: the coefficient system matters.',
 ['Use an oriented cycle with m vertices and m edges over a field k. Put k at every vertex and edge, choose nonzero r_j∈k, and define (δs)_j=s_{j+1}−r_js_j, with indices modulo m. Set H⁰=kerδ, H¹=cokerδ, and h=∏r_j.'],
 'Holonomy criterion for a local system on the cycle',
 'If h=1, both H⁰ and H¹ have dimension one. If h≠1, both vanish. The underlying graph still has an ordinary cycle in either case.',
 'A global section satisfies s_{j+1}=r_js_j. Propagating once around the cycle gives s₀=hs₀. If h≠1 this forces s₀=0 and hence all s_j=0. If h=1 every choice of s₀ propagates consistently, giving a one-dimensional kernel. Since δ is a square m×m matrix, its cokernel and kernel have equal dimensions by rank-nullity. This proves the complete statement without assuming the transports can be globally trivialized. Rescaling vertex coordinates changes individual r_j but leaves their product invariant. In a higher-dimensional cell complex, restriction maps must also satisfy the face-composition identities that make the cochain differential square to zero; arbitrary matrices called transports need not define a sheaf complex. Likewise a cup product needs compatible bilinear maps of coefficient systems, rather than following from linear stalks alone.',
 'Take a three-cycle over Q. First use r=(2,3,1/6), then r=(2,3,1).',
 'For the first choice, s=(1,2,6) is a nonzero global section and spans H⁰; h=1, so dimH¹=1. For the second, h=6, and the equations s₁=2s₀,s₂=3s₁,s₀=s₂ imply5s₀=0, hence δ is invertible over Q and both groups vanish.',
 'Global compatibility is detected by the product around the loop, with a fully specified coefficient convention.',
 'This is a rank-one local system on a graph. It does not identify an unretrieved higher-dimensional Morphonic sheaf or justify arbitrary claimed multiplication laws.',
 'Construct an explicit two-dimensional cellular sheaf, verify every face-composition square, and compute how monodromy and two-cell attachment change its cohomology.')
add(23,'Koszul','Total-complex signs and mapping cones are structural data','Chain complexes',
 'Which sign makes two commuting differentials into a total differential, and why does the mapping cone test chain-map compatibility?',
 'The retrieved July discussion includes double complexes, Koszul signs and mapping cones. These operations become rigorous only after fixing degrees and signs. A small rational example exposes the failure that an unsigned sum would conceal.',
 ['Let C^{p,q} have horizontal δ:C^{p,q}→C^{p+1,q} and vertical ∂:C^{p,q}→C^{p,q+1}, with δ²=∂²=0 and δ∂=∂δ. Total degree is p+q. For a cochain map f:C→D, Cone(f)^n=D^n⊕C^{n+1}.'],
 'Signed totalization and cone criterion',
 'D_tot=δ+(−1)^p∂ satisfies D_tot²=0. The cone differential d(d,c)=(d_Dd+fc,−d_Cc) squares to zero precisely when f commutes with the differentials.',
 'On bidegree(p,q), the mixed term from δ followed by the signed vertical map has sign(−1)^{p+1}, whereas the reverse mixed term has sign(−1)^p. They cancel because δ and ∂ commute; their pure squares vanish. In the cone, applying d twice gives first component d_D²d+(d_Df−fd_C)c and second component d_C²c. Thus the only possible obstruction is exactly the chain-map defect. The shift and its minus sign are indispensable. A cone records whether f is a quasi-isomorphism: its long exact cohomology sequence follows by taking the short exact sequence D→Cone(f)→C[1] and tracing cocycle representatives. In particular Cone(id) is contractible; an explicit homotopy is h(d,c)=(0,d), since dh+hd=(d,c).',
 'Tensor two complexes Q --id→ Q in degrees0 and1.',
 'The total groups have dimensions1,2,1. With degree-one coordinates ordered(horizontal,vertical), d⁰(1)=(1,1) and d¹(a,b)=−a+b. Their product is zero, and ker d¹=span(1,1)=im d⁰, so the total complex is acyclic. Using the unsigned map a+b instead gives d¹d⁰(1)=2, which fails over Q.',
 'Signs can be verified on exact matrices and give a real compatibility test rather than decorative notation.',
 'The convention assumes commuting raw differentials. If the starting bicomplex already anticommutes, one uses a different totalization convention. No full higher-coherence structure is recovered from this example.',
 'Build the proposed Morphonic bicomplex as explicit graded matrices and verify its total differential, cone homotopies and induced connecting maps over the intended coefficient ring.')
add(24,'adjunction','Tensor–Hom adjunction separates currying from dualizability','Linear algebra and category theory',
 'What does tensor–Hom adjunction prove for arbitrary vector spaces, and which extra finiteness hypothesis produces a dual?',
 'The source’s tensor, internal-Hom and adjunction language has a concrete linear model. Currying works for arbitrary algebraic vector spaces. A coevaluation satisfying both triangle identities is a stronger requirement and forces finite dimension.',
 ['All tensor products are algebraic over a field k. Hom denotes all linear maps. A duality datum for V consists of a vector space W, evaluation ev:W⊗V→k and coevaluation coev:k→V⊗W satisfying the two triangle identities.'],
 'Adjunction and the finite-dimensional duality criterion',
 'Hom(V⊗W,U)≅Hom(V,Hom(W,U)) naturally, without a dimension restriction. A vector space V is dualizable in this tensor category if and only if V is finite-dimensional.',
 'Given F:V⊗W→U, send v to the map w↦F(v⊗w). Conversely a linear map v↦G_v gives the bilinear pairing(v,w)↦G_v(w), which factors uniquely through V⊗W. The constructions are inverse by evaluation on pure tensors; naturality follows by composition. For finite-dimensional V with basis e_i and dual basis e^i, take W=V*, ev(φ⊗v)=φ(v) and coev(1)=Σe_i⊗e^i. Either triangle identity reduces to v=Σe^i(v)e_i. Conversely every algebraic tensor coev(1) is a finite sum Σ_{i=1}^r v_i⊗w_i. The triangle identity then writes every v as Σv_i ev(w_i⊗v), so V is spanned by finitely many v_i. This proves necessity and explains why a formal infinite basis sum is not an algebraic tensor.',
 'Take V=Q², W=Q³, U=Q and F(v⊗w)=vᵀMw with M=[[1,2,3],[4,5,6]].',
 'Currying sends v=(a,b) to the functional with coefficients(a+4b,2a+5b,3a+6b). For v=(2,−1), this is(−2,−1,0), giving F(v⊗(1,2,3))=−4 by either route. Coevaluation for Q² uses exactly e₁⊗e¹+e₂⊗e² and reconstructs(2,−1).',
 'The adjunction is an explicit universal-property statement; duality adds a finite-rank identity certificate.',
 'Topological tensor products and continuous duals belong to different categories and require separate hypotheses. This proof does not establish unspecified enriched or higher adjunctions.',
 'Specify the actual objects, morphisms and monoidal product before proposing higher duality, then test the triangle identities on a finite chain-complex model.')
add(25,'y^2+y','The elliptic curve of discriminant37 admits exact group-law and rank-lower-bound certificates','Elliptic curves',
 'Which exact computations on y²+y=x³−x prove that a visible point has infinite order, and what still requires a descent?',
 'The July29 source reports multiples, a 2-descent and rank-one conclusions for the conductor37 curve. Exact group arithmetic and reductions modulo small primes give a self-contained rank lower bound. The stronger Selmer and Tate–Shafarevich claims remain conditional on an unavailable descent certificate.',
 ['Let E/Q be y²+y=x³−x with identity O at infinity and P=(0,0). Negation is−(x,y)=(x,−1−y). The discriminant is Δ=37. A prime is good when it does not divideΔ.'],
 'Infinite order without adopting the reported descent',
 'E(Q) has trivial torsion and P has infinite order; consequently its rank is at least one. The displayed calculation alone does not prove rank exactly one.',
 'For distinct points use slopeλ=(y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁); doubling uses λ=(3x₁²−1)/(2y₁+1). Then x₃=λ²−x₁−x₂ and y₃=−y₁−1+λ(x₁−x₃). These formulas give exact rational multiples. Counting solutions over F₃ and F₅ gives group orders7 and8, respectively. Good reduction injects the prime-to-p rational torsion into E(F_p). Any torsion prime other than3,5 would therefore divide gcd(7,8)=1. A 3-primary torsion point would inject at5 and require3|8; a 5-primary one would inject at3 and require5|7. Neither is possible, so the torsion subgroup is trivial. P is a finite point and hence nonzero, proving infinite order. This argument supplies no upper rank bound. A certified 2-Selmer calculation of dimension one, together with the found point and no rational2-torsion, would supply that missing bound.',
 'Compute P through6P, and transform the equation for a 2-division check.',
 'The multiples are2P=(1,0),3P=(−1,−1),4P=(2,−3),5P=(1/4,−5/8),6P=(6,14). With X=4x,Y=8y+4 the equation becomes Y²=X³−16X+16. Its cubic has no rational root, consistently with no rational2-torsion; the exact torsion argument above is stronger.',
 'The visible orbit is an exact constructive result, while descent claims are kept distinct from it.',
 'No covering curves, local-solubility witnesses or Selmer basis were recovered. The source’s exact rank and Sha[2] assertions are not certified here.',
 'Return a complete2-descent packet with étale-algebra representatives, local tests and covering maps, then combine its upper bound with the explicit infinite-order point.',[EC])
add(26,'[91','Riemann–Roch proves a code bound, not an unexhibited minimum-weight word','Algebraic-geometric coding theory',
 'Assuming the reported maximal genus-three curve exists, which code parameters and decoding radius follow rigorously?',
 'The July31 assistant source gives a genus-three curve over F₄₉,92 rational points and a code[91,37,52]. The curve equation, point list and decoder were not recovered. A conditional theorem proves the guaranteed dimension and distance bound without promoting an asserted exact distance into a demonstrated one.',
 ['Let X/F_q be a smooth projective geometrically integral curve of genus g. Choose distinct rational points P₁,…,P_n and a divisor G disjoint from them. The evaluation code is C_L(D,G)={ (f(P₁),…,f(P_n)):f∈L(G)}, with D=ΣP_i.'],
 'One-point Goppa parameter certificate',
 'If2g−2<degG<n, then dimC_L=degG+1−g and its minimum distance is at least n−degG. Thus a genus-three curve with92 rational points yields a one-point code[91,37,d≥52] usingG=39P_∞ and the other91 points.',
 'The kernel of evaluation is L(G−D). Its divisor degree is negative, so only zero belongs to that space: a nonzero function has principal divisor degree zero and cannot satisfy a negative-degree effectivity condition. Evaluation is therefore injective. Riemann–Roch and degG>2g−2 giveℓ(G)=degG+1−g. A nonzero f∈L(G) has at mostdegG zeros among the evaluation points, since its zero divisor and pole divisor have the same degree and its poles are bounded byG. Its evaluation has weight at least n−degG. These arguments prove a lower bound; equality additionally needs an f attaining exactlydegG evaluation zeros. The Hasse–Weil upper bound atq49,g3 is49+1+2·3·7=92, so the reported count would indeed be maximal, provided the actual curve and count are verified.',
 'Work conditionally with q=49,g=3,N=92,n=91 and degG=39.',
 'The dimension is39+1−3=37 and the designed distance is91−39=52. A nearest-codeword decoder is uniquely defined for every received word within⌊(52−1)/2⌋=25 errors of a codeword. An algorithm reported only through24 errors may have a weaker guarantee; that does not contradict the distance bound. It does not establish an efficient25-error algorithm either.',
 'The construction separates existence, parameter bounds, exact distance and algorithmic decoding into independent mathematical obligations.',
 'The actual curve and decoder remain unavailable. Larger reported tower-code tuples likewise need explicit function fields, evaluation divisors and certificates.',
 'Publish the curve over F₇[u]/(u²+1), its92 points and an evaluation basis, then exhibit a weight52 codeword or prove the true distance is larger.')
add(27,'canonical base-12','Base twelve changes representation while preserving exact arithmetic','Number representation',
 'Which rational numbers have finite duodecimal expansions, and how are periodic expansions certified?',
 'The July31 language discussion includes base twelve and symbolic representation. Its mathematical core is a division algorithm, not a change in the underlying rational numbers. Exact remainder orbits give terminating and repeating expansions with unambiguous conventions.',
 ['Use digits0,…,9,A,B for values0,…,11. For a rational0≤a/b<1 in lowest terms, long division sets r₀=a,12r_j=b d_{j+1}+r_{j+1}, with0≤r_{j+1}<b. A bar denotes infinite repetition.'],
 'Termination and periodicity criterion',
 'The base-twelve expansion of a/b terminates exactly when b divides12^N for some N, equivalently b has no prime factors except2 and3. Every rational has an eventually periodic expansion; after removing the2- and3-parts of the denominator, the period divides the multiplicative order of12 modulo its remaining part.',
 'AfterN steps the division identities telescope to a/b=Σ_{j=1}^N d_j12^{-j}+r_N/(b12^N). Termination is equivalent to r_N=0 and hence to b|12^N because gcd(a,b)=1. Conversely that divisibility forces zero remainder. Only finitely many remainders occur, so a nonzero remainder must eventually repeat and the deterministic update then repeats forever. When the denominator is coprime to12, the remainder is12^j a modb and the first return occurs at the multiplicative order of12. Powers of2 and3 contribute only a finite preperiod. An expansion ending in infinitely manyB digits is the familiar alternate representation of a terminating number; choosing the non-B-tail convention restores uniqueness.',
 'Represent2026,1/8 and1/5 in base twelve.',
 '2026=1·12³+2·12²+0·12+10, so it is120A₁₂. Also.16₁₂=1/12+6/144=1/8. For1/5 the remainders follow1→2→4→3→1 and the digits are2,4,9,7. Thus1/5=.overline{2497}₁₂; equivalently(2·12³+4·12²+9·12+7)/(12⁴−1)=4147/20735=1/5.',
 'Each symbolic statement reduces to an integer identity and a finite remainder certificate.',
 'This does not support claims that a base or symbolic language changes numerical truth or objectively improves perception. Those would require separately defined criteria.',
 'Specify a concrete coding cost for a symbolic arithmetic language and compare bases by provable representation length and exact arithmetic complexity rather than aesthetic analogy.')
add(28,'Graeffe','A root atlas combines Rouché counts with explicit root squaring','Polynomial root certification',
 'How can a coefficient inequality and a Graeffe step certify root counts without pretending to give universal radical formulas?',
 'The July31 source proposes square-free reduction, Pellet/Graeffe filters and a universal polynomial-solving atlas. A useful classical atlas certifies where roots are and how many occur. It does not bypass the obstruction to general radical formulas.',
 ['For p(c+rw)=Σ_{j=0}^n b_jw^j, a disk means|z−c|<r and roots are counted with multiplicity. Write p(z)=E(z²)+zO(z²), with even and odd parts separated.'],
 'Dominant-term disk count and Graeffe identity',
 'If|b_k|>Σ_{j≠k}|b_j|, then p has exactly k roots in the disk. For leading coefficienta and rootsr_i counted with multiplicity, G(y)=E(y)²−yO(y)²=(−1)^n a²∏(y−r_i²). Thus its roots are the squares of those ofp, with multiplicities.',
 'On|w|=1 the non-k terms have magnitude at mostΣ_{j≠k}|b_j|, strictly below|b_kw^k|. Rouché’s theorem therefore gives the same zero count as the monomial b_kw^k, namelyk. Strictness also proves the boundary is root-free. For the second statement, p(z)p(−z)=E(z²)²−z²O(z²)². Factoring p(z)=a∏(z−r_i) gives p(z)p(−z)=(−1)^n a²∏(z²−r_i²). Substituting y=z² proves the claim and the sign. Root squaring separates distinct root moduli, but may merge roots of opposite signs and amplify coefficient ranges. It is a filtering tool; square-free factorization and subsequent certified isolation remain necessary.',
 'Use p(z)=z³−4z+1 for disk counts and q(z)=z²−3z+2 for root squaring.',
 'On|z|=1,4>1+1 proves one root inside. On|z|=3,27>12+1 proves all three inside; hence exactly two lie in1<|z|<3. Forq, E(y)=y+2 and O(y)=−3, soG(y)=(y+2)²−9y=y²−5y+4, with roots1 and4, the squares of1 and2.',
 'The output is a finite exact counting certificate with stated disks and multiplicities.',
 'No original all-degree solver or test corpus was recovered. These sufficient tests can be inconclusive, especially near equal-modulus clusters; inconclusive does not mean root-free.',
 'Combine exact square-free factorization, adaptive rational disks, Graeffe filters and certified local Newton steps, returning an independently checkable disjoint root cover.')
add(29,'universal','A contraction certificate gives a genuinely guaranteed Newton enclosure','Validated nonlinear solving',
 'What finite inequalities prove existence and uniqueness of a root in a chosen interval?',
 'The late-July universal-solving discussion is made constructive by replacing an unqualified convergence promise with a local certificate. A fixed inverse-Jacobian approximation produces a contraction whose hypotheses can be checked with rational bounds.',
 ['Let f be continuously differentiable on I=[c−r,c+r], choose a nonzero constant C, and set T(x)=x−Cf(x). Assume sup_I|1−Cf′(x)|≤q<1.'],
 'One-dimensional interval Newton–Banach certificate',
 'If|Cf(c)|+qr≤r, then f has exactly one root inI. Starting anywhere inI, iteratingT converges to that root with geometric contraction factorq.',
 'The derivative bound and the mean-value theorem give|T(x)−T(y)|≤q|x−y|. Moreover|T(x)−c|≤|T(c)−c|+q|x−c|≤|Cf(c)|+qr≤r, soT maps the closed interval into itself. Completeness and the contraction theorem give a unique fixed point. SinceC≠0, fixed points are exactly zeros off. The same argument bounds the distance from an iterate to the root by|T(x)−x|/(1−q). Every hypothesis is local and explicit. A convergence plot or small residual is weaker: for example a nearly singular derivative can make a tiny residual coexist with a substantial root error. In several variables the analogous test uses a norm bound forI−CJF throughout a box and an inclusion test for its centered image.',
 'Certify the positive root off(x)=x²−2 usingc=7/5,C=5/14 andr=1/50.',
 'OnI=[69/50,71/50], T′=1−5x/7 ranges from1/70 to−1/70, so q=1/70. Also|Cf(c)|=1/70. The inclusion bound is1/70+1/3500=51/3500<1/50=70/3500. Thus there is exactly one root in[1.38,1.42], and the fixed-inverse iteration converges from every point of this interval. The negative root lies outside it.',
 'Existence, uniqueness, enclosure and a convergence rate are certified by exact inequalities.',
 'This local theorem does not guarantee finding a valid box for every polynomial or remove global root-separation costs. The source’s universal algorithm was not retrieved as a complete implementation.',
 'Use an exact global root count to seed intervals, then attach such certificates to every isolated simple root and a separate multiplicity-aware certificate to repeated roots.')

add(30,'Gauss–Bonnet','Discrete Gauss–Bonnet is an exact angle-counting identity','Finite geometry and topology',
 'How does a local angular defect recover Euler characteristic on a triangulated surface?',
 'The July25 finite-topology register names Gauss–Bonnet alongside homology and transport. Its simplest complete version is a piecewise Euclidean surface theorem. It connects local geometric data with an independently countable topological invariant and supplies an exact baseline for any richer representation.',
 ['Let a finite triangulation of a closed surface haveV vertices,E edges andF faces. Give each face a nondegenerate Euclidean triangle metric, with matching lengths across glued edges. At a vertexv define angular defectκ_v=2π−Σ_{f∋v}θ_{f,v}. Euler characteristic isχ=V−E+F.'],
 'Polyhedral Gauss–Bonnet',
 'The total angular defect isΣ_vκ_v=2πχ. No orientability assumption is needed for this counting form of the theorem.',
 'Every Euclidean face contributes exactlyπ when its three angles are added. ThusΣ_vκ_v=2πV−πF. Because the surface is closed, every edge is incident to two triangular faces, so3F=2E. Substitute this relation into2π(V−E+F) to obtain2πV−πF, proving the identity. Both the local-angle sum and global incidence counts are essential. A mesh with boundary has boundary edges incident to one face and requires an additional boundary turning term; the closed formula cannot simply be copied. The theorem is insensitive to how curvature is redistributed among vertices while the incidence topology stays fixed. It does not say that every assignment of defects summing to2πχ is realizable by a chosen edge-length metric.',
 'Take the boundary of a regular tetrahedron, with four equilateral triangular faces.',
 'HereV=4,E=6,F=4 andχ=2. Exactly three face anglesπ/3 meet at each vertex, so each angular defect isπ. The total is4π=2π·2. Subdividing a flat face by adding an interior vertex creates no new total defect: the interior angle sum is2π and the surrounding old angles still add to the original three face angles.',
 'A topology–geometry bridge is certified by elementary incidence and angle identities rather than a visual resemblance.',
 'No original Morphonic mesh, edge lengths or curvature table was recovered. This theorem provides a constructive finite baseline, not a check of an unseen realization.',
 'Publish a piecewise Euclidean complex with exact angle or interval enclosures, verify all edge gluings and compare its defect measure with a refined discrete connection.')
add(31,'rationals/reals/p-adics','A p-adic completion changes convergence while keeping rational identities','Valuations and exact lifting',
 'How can the same rational series diverge in the real metric yet converge p-adically, and how is a p-adic root built exactly?',
 'The July31 representation discussion includes rationals, reals and p-adics. A valuation makes this distinction precise: it changes which approximations are small, while preserving ordinary algebraic equalities on the embedded rational field. Root lifting provides a constructive rather than purely metaphorical example.',
 ['For a primep and nonzero rationalx, letv_p(x) be the exponent ofp in its factorization and|x|_p=p^{-v_p(x)}; set|0|_p=0. Q_p is the completion ofQ in this metric. Congruence modulo p^n records an error ofp-adic norm at mostp^{-n}.'],
 'Geometric convergence and simple-root lifting',
 'The seriesΣ_{j≥0}p^j converges inQ_p to1/(1−p). If an integer polynomialf hasf(r₁)≡0 modp andf′(r₁)≠0 modp, its root lifts uniquely through compatible congruencesr_n modulo p^n, producing a root inZ_p.',
 'The finite geometric identity(1−p)Σ_{j=0}^{N−1}p^j=1−p^N is valid overQ. Since|p^N|_p=p^{-N}→0, completeness gives the stated limit; the real terms instead grow and their partial sums diverge. For lifting supposef(r_n)≡0 modp^n andtryr_{n+1}=r_n+t p^n with0≤t<p. Taylor expansion modulo p^{n+1} givesf(r_{n+1})≡f(r_n)+t p^n f′(r_n). Dividing byp^n leaves one linear equation fort modulop. The derivative is a unit modulo p, so that equation has exactly one solution. The compatible sequence is Cauchy and continuity of polynomial evaluation gives a root. Uniqueness applies to the root in the chosen residue class, not every root ofthe polynomial.',
 'Takep=5 andf(x)=x²−6, starting withr₁=1.',
 'The successive least residues are r₂=16 modulo25, r₃=16 modulo125 andr₄=516 modulo625. Their squares are6 modulo the respective moduli, and each new residue agrees with the previous one. The other root begins in the residue class−1, so the construction does not claim global uniqueness.',
 'Exact modular corrections build an infinite object with a proved convergence metric.',
 'A p-adic root need not be a rational number or correspond to an ordinary real approximation. No source-specific p-adic curve or regulator computation is inferred.',
 'Construct a compatible p-adic representation layer with explicit precision propagation, proving how addition, multiplication and simple-root lifting preserve the advertised error bounds.')

